A||||13||batboy||rear clunking noise||||1
Z||000000||batboy||10-04-1999||10:22 PM||||Every now and then as I'm starting out in my 85 GT (with a 4 speed) I hear a clunk from the rear of the car. At first I thought it was my toolbox shifting around in the trunk. I removed that and still the clunk once in a while. The dogbone was bad, so I installed a new ultimate doggyboner. Still have the occasional clunk. Would worn CV joints do this? On FWD cars, worn CV joints usually click when you turn a corner. This is a single thud or clunk. Any other ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000001||lowCG||10-04-1999||10:49 PM||||They won't click,because they're in a fixed position;no steering going on,but if the CVs are loose enough to hear a clunk,you may be able to give the twist test for slop.||||reg||1
Z||000002||rcn491||10-04-1999||10:59 PM||||I suggest you take a look at your tansaxle mounts. A bad dogdone will allow excessive strain on these mounts.||||reg||1
Z||000003||mwbackus||10-04-1999||11:49 PM||||On pre-88 Fieros, worn cradle bushings are the first place to look.<P>Mike||||reg||1
Z||000004||SCCA FIERO||10-05-1999||01:31 AM||||My guess is the front transaxle mount. Mine clunks too and it's the front mount (the 70 dollar one compared to 20 for the rear one)||||reg||9
Z||000005||Cliff Pennock||10-05-1999||03:26 AM||||I had a "clunk" coming from the back of my Fiero. It took me two years to find out where the sound came from (just found out this weekend). It was coming from <B>inside</B> the decklid...||||reg||1
Z||000006||88 Fiero||10-05-1999||10:29 AM||||bat..I think you should check your tranny mounts...when that breaks...the tranny moves a lot when you shift gears...also Chris West of West Coast Fieros sells Tranny mounts that works awsome on Fieros...it's been box-up pretty good!!!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Patrick||10-05-1999||02:02 PM||||<BR>When I change gears with my 5-speed under hard acceleration, I have to compensate with the steering wheel to keep my Fiero going in a straight line. When I first got the car and it did this, I thought I must have been causing this problem by turning the steering wheel a bit when I was shifting. I finally realized that it was not me, but it was the car all by itself that was changing directions during the shifts.<P>This is probably broken or loose mounts allowing the motor/transaxle to shift around and to then change somewhat the rear wheel alignment. Does this make any sense?   ||||reg||5
Z||000008||88 Fiero||10-05-1999||03:28 PM||||Patrick...on my old '85 with a 4-Speed tranny..I have that experience..everytime I shift gear I could hear a clunk in the rear and after I had it check it turn out my tranny mount is busted...||||reg||1
Z||000009||theogre||10-05-1999||03:58 PM||||inside the lid cliff????? let me guess.... some fool at pontiac left you an extra wing bolt.<P>Pat, the steering problem you descibe, called torque steer, is most often caused by the changing of load on the rear control arms and/or cradle bushings. The engine/tranny movment isn't translated to the wheels except thru the cradle. If the cradle bushings or rear A arm bushings are soft, they can't hold the things in place as the load shifts. In stick cars these loads don't change as smoothly as automatics so the bushings take a bigger pounding.<P>plus if the cradle bushings are soft, the position of the torque strut(dog bone) becomes even more important. If the strut is off on one side, like Fiero is, then the engine movement can easily rotate the cradle around its vertical center(Y axis). Not a good thing when trying to keep a car in a straight line.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-05-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||Patrick||10-05-1999||05:09 PM||||<BR>Ogre, thanks for the explanation. Does this mean that there is something worn (bushings?) that definitely needs replacing, or is this phenomenon just something that needs to be accepted? I might add that this "torque steer" isn't too bad to have to deal with.||||reg||5
Z||000011||Cliff Pennock||10-05-1999||05:50 PM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Ogre, I'm not kidding! I had all kinds of stuff replaced to get rid of this knock but nothing seemed to help and I gave up on it. Until a few days ago - I was working on the EGR - when I shut the decklid I heared <B>the</B> clunk! I moved the decklid up and down fast a few times and there it was. I have no idea what's inside to make this noise, it's not like there's a loose bolt or anything... As soon as the weather clears up a bit here (expected in 2006  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ) I'll remove the decklid-lock and see what could be causing this.||||reg||10
Z||000012||theogre||10-05-1999||08:15 PM||||2006 assuming El Ninio doesn't get you.    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> <P>Fixing the torque steer depends on just whats flaky. redoing the cradle bushings would be a good start I think. The Prothane polyurathane ones are hard as stones. (I just got mine today.... With both hands I can't squish them even a little.)<P>If you can't do it all at once. I'd do the cradle then the A-arms. do the ball joints when you do the A-arms. Make sure the dog bone is good. It doesn't effect torque steer much, but it can break exhaust parts and motor mounts if it's bad.<P>BTW.... rear CV's definatly will click. one of mine does. the CV's are not in a straight line most of the time. they don't flex front to back much but they do go up and down.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 10-05-1999).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||batboy||10-10-1999||12:30 PM||||I finally got around to jacking up the rear of my GT and crawled under there with a pry bar. I have a lower motor mount that's separated (right side of car under the pulleys and belts). The tranny mounts don't look so great either. Looks like another project to do. Thanks for all the helpful suggestions.||||reg||1
